EXTENSION "ArM5_Magi_of_Hermes_AlexanderOfJerbiton_Sidebars" {
    NAME "Ars Magica Fifth Edition, Magi of Hermes - Chapter One: Alexander of Jerbiton sidebars"
    VERSION "0.5.0"
    SPEC_VERSION "0.5"
    RELEASE_DATE "2026-10-08"
    DEPENDS_ON "ArM5_Magi_of_Hermes_Core_Ext"

    # The chapter's non-record boxes as GUIDANCE with verbatim TEXT (story seeds, rules
    # notes, background boxes; list and sub-heading units included).

    GUIDANCE {
        ENTRY ^"Equipment for Alexander’s Attendants" #tb47c3a7fd782b012570f13 {
            TEXT "Equipment for Alexander’s Attendants\n\nAlexander’s attendants are often transformed into animals. It is vital that his party have equipment that does not require requisites to transform.\n\n**Armor:** Alexander’s attendants usually wear quilted armor (**ArM5** page 176) made of woolen thread. An expert armorer would quickly determine that the armor had been made so that small pieces of metal that are usual in its construction have been replaced with pieces of bone.\n\n**Weapons:** Alexander’s grogs often lose their weapons when they change shape, if they are forced to flee. To compensate for this, some of his warriors carry large fighting knives and clubs made from animal bone, horn, or ivory with leather and beeswax grips. These weapons do –2 Damage compared to their steel counterparts in the tables on pages 176 and 177 of **ArM5**, except daggers and knives, which do –1 Damage. All of these weapons are likely to break on botches.\n\n**Water:** The greatest flaw in Alexander’s transformations is that the water his grogs are carrying, in leather skins, does not transform when the skins do, and so it is always lost. He has tried various solutions to this problem, but none have proven promising. His troops always carry a little mead or kumis (fermented honey or milk), but that offers no protection against dehydration."
        }
    }

}
